### What is the universal rule for sealed-type location?

All permitted subtypes must be in the same module as the sealed type. A sealed hierarchy is a closed, author-controlled design, and allowing it to cross module boundaries would let another module insert itself into your closed family. The compiler never permits a subtype from a different module.

```java
// module-info.java for com.bank.accounts
module com.bank.accounts {
    exports com.bank.accounts.api;
    // com.bank.accounts.products is NOT exported (internal)
}
```

### How do packages work within a named module?

Within a named module, permitted subtypes may be in different packages. The module is a strong encapsulation boundary, so the author controls everything inside it.

```java
// In package com.bank.accounts.api (exported)
package com.bank.accounts.api;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits com.bank.accounts.products.SavingsAccount,
                com.bank.accounts.products.CurrentAccount,
                com.bank.accounts.products.LoanAccount {
    String id();
    BigDecimal balance();
}
```

```java
// In package com.bank.accounts.products (internal, not exported)
package com.bank.accounts.products;

import com.bank.accounts.api.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    private final String id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    public SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    @Override public String id() { return id; }
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }
}
```

The sealed interface is exported for callers, but the concrete products are internal. This is a clean architectural pattern.

### What happens on the classpath (unnamed module)?

On the classpath, all permitted subtypes must be in the same package as the sealed type. Without a module boundary, the package is the only encapsulation unit the compiler can rely on.

```java
// All in the same package: com.bank.model
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return new BigDecimal("500000");
    }
}

final class CurrentAccount implements Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal balance() {
        return new BigDecimal("250000");
    }
}
```

```java
// WRONG: different package on classpath
// package com.bank.products;
// final class LoanAccount implements com.bank.model.Account { }
// COMPILE ERROR: class is in a different package from its sealed supertype
```

### Why are cross-module sealed hierarchies forbidden?

If a sealed hierarchy could span modules, an external vendor module could inject a new permitted subtype into your closed set, defeating the entire purpose. The compiler and JVM both enforce same-module containment.

```java
// module com.vendor.extensions
// CANNOT add a subtype of com.bank.accounts.api.Account
// because Account is sealed in module com.bank.accounts

// final class VendorProduct implements Account { }
// COMPILE ERROR: not in the same module as the sealed type
```

### How does the JVM validate the rule at load time?

The compiler records the permitted subtypes in the `PermittedSubclasses` class-file attribute. The JVM checks at class loading that each subtype actually extends the sealed type and is in the same module. This prevents bytecode tricks from smuggling in cross-module subtypes.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ModuleValidationDemo {
    sealed interface Account permits Savings, Current {}
    final record Savings() implements Account {}
    final record Current() implements Account {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sealed: " + Account.class.isSealed());
        System.out.println("Module: " + Account.class.getModule());
        Arrays.stream(Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses())
                .forEach(c -> System.out.println(
                        "  " + c.getSimpleName() + " in " + c.getModule()));
    }
}
```

### What are the practical consequences?

Modularising your application unlocks cleaner package organisation for sealed hierarchies. A library author shipping a modular JAR can spread a sealed type and its subtypes across several packages for readability, exporting only the API package. A non-modular application on the classpath must keep the whole family in one package.

| Environment | Package Rule | Cross-Module |
|---|---|---|
| Named module | Different packages OK (same module) | Never allowed |
| Classpath | Same package required | Never allowed |

### When should you modularise for sealed hierarchies?

When you have a large sealed domain model (many account products, transaction types, or regulatory states), modularising lets you organise the subtypes across clean packages while maintaining the closed-set guarantee. This is exactly what production banking systems need.

```java
// Module layout:
// com.bank.accounts/
//   com.bank.accounts.api/       (exported)
//     Account.java               (sealed interface)
//   com.bank.accounts.products/  (internal)
//     SavingsAccount.java        (final)
//     CurrentAccount.java        (final)
//     LoanAccount.java           (sealed)
//   com.bank.accounts.loans/     (internal)
//     HomeLoan.java              (final)
//     CarLoan.java               (final)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The same-module rule is enforced both at compile time and at load time through the `PermittedSubclasses` attribute. You cannot smuggle in a cross-module subtype through bytecode manipulation. This means sealed hierarchies have genuine integrity within the module boundary. The module system and sealed types reinforce each other: modules provide encapsulation, and sealing provides a closed type family within that encapsulation.
